{{also|décide|décidé}}
==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*kh₂eyd-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|deciden}}, from {{der|en|fro|decider}}, from {{der|en|la|dēcīdere}}, infinitive of {{m|la|dēcīdō|t=cut off, decide}}, from {{m|la|dē|t=down from}} + {{m|la|caedō|t=cut}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/dɪˈsaɪd/}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-decide.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|aɪd|s=2}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} To [[resolve]] (a [[contest]], [[problem]], [[dispute]], etc.); to [[choose]], [[determine]], or [[settle]].
#: {{syn|en|choose|determine|pick|elect|Thesaurus:choose}}
#: {{ux|en|The election will be '''decided''' on foreign policies.}}
#: {{ux|en|It was '''decided''' to meet here at midnight.}}
#: {{ux|en|Her last-minute goal '''decided''' the game.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 6-1|IV|i|passage=The quarrel toucheth none but us alone; / Betwixt ourselves let us '''decide''' it then.}}
#* {{quote-book
|en
|date=December 21, 1987
|author=w:Richard Nixon
|title=Letters to Trump
|publisher=Winning Team Publishing
|year_published=2023
|ISBN=9781735503752
|OCLC=1375593143
|page=10
|text=I did not see the program, but Mrs. Nixon told me that you were great on the Donahue Show.<br>As you can imagine, she is an expert on politics and she predicts that whenever you '''decide''' to run for office you will be a winner!}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To make a [[judgment]], especially after [[deliberation]].
#: {{syn|en|judge|make up one's mind|Thesaurus:decide}}
#: {{ux|en|You must '''decide''' between good and evil.}}
#: {{ux|en|I have '''decided''' that it is healthier to walk to work.}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|1 Kings|20|40|passage=So shall thy judgment be; thyself hast '''decided''' it.}}
#* {{quote-book|en||url=https://www.google.com/books/edition/Downside_by_and_Large/C5YKAAAAMAAJ?hl=en&gbpv=1&bsq=%22a+flutter+of+moths%22&dq=%22a+flutter+of+moths%22&printsec=frontcover|title=Downside By and Large|passage=Since this cowl released a flutter of moths and parted at the shoulder as he was getting into it, he '''decided''' that it must be his own.|page=277|year=1954|author=Hubert Van Zeller}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To cause someone to come to a [[decision]].
#: {{ux|en|Your admonition '''decide'''d me against my intended [[course of action]].}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1920|author=[[w:Arthur_Conan_Doyle|Sir Arthur Conan Doyle]]|title=The Adventure of the Three Gables|publisher=Norton|year_published=2005|page=1537|passage=It '''decides''' me to look into the matter, for if it is worth anyone's while to take so much trouble, there must be something in it.}}
# {{lb|en|computability theory}} Of a [[Turing machine#Noun|Turing machine]]: to return a correct answer (for some yes-or-no problem) on every possible [[input#Noun|input]].
#: {{ux|en|No Turing Machine can '''decide''' the [[halting problem#Noun|halting problem]].}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Michael Sipser|title=Introduction to the Theory of Computation|location=Boston, M.A.|publisher=PWS Publishing Company|year=1997|page=313|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/isbn_0534956513/page/313/mode/1up|isbn=0534956513|passage=First, the hierarchy theorems tell us that a Turing machine can '''decide''' more languages in EXPSPACE than it can in PSPACE.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} To [[cut off]]; to [[separate]].
#: {{syn|en|divorce|sever|sunder|Thesaurus:divide}}
#* {{RQ:Fuller Holy State|passage=Our seat denies us traffic here; / The sea, too near, '''decides''' us from the rest.}}

